<div style="background-color: #e0f2f7; padding: 20px; border-radius: 8px; text-align: center; border: 2px solid #1a5276;">
    <h1 style="color: #1a5276; margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ____ – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="color: #1a5276; font-size: 1.1em; margin-top: 10px;"><em>Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</em></p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> The Importance of Water</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> Clean Water Is Important Because...</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td colspan="3" style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Interactive: Discuss personal future plans. Productive: Develop simple arguments based on texts studied.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can discuss personal future plans to help save water (e.g., "I will use clean water for cooking.")<br>Can develop simple arguments about the importance of water conservation (e.g., "Water is an important resource in Panama. If we protect our rivers and streams, we will have fresh water for a longer time.")<br>Can express ideas about water-related projects in class discussions (e.g., "Our ideas to save water are: 1. Turn off the tap. 2. Collect rainwater.").</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f0f8ff; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays various pictures of water use (e.g., someone drinking water, washing dishes, a running tap, a clean river, a polluted river). Students look at the pictures and quickly shout out words associated with them (e.g., "drink," "waste," "river," "clean," "polluted," "tap"). The teacher asks, "What do these pictures tell us about water?" to elicit initial ideas about its importance and problems. Students practice pronunciation of "water" and "important" focusing on syllabification.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models sentences using the first conditional and 'for + -ing' related to the pictures. For example, pointing to a running tap picture, the teacher says: "If we waste water like this, there will be less potable water for us. We use water for drinking and cooking." The teacher emphasizes the stress on "important" and "forecast" (if introduced), and models the cause-effect structure, "If we waste water, there will be less for everyone."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the target structures.
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I turn off the tap, what will happen?" (Expected answer: "We will save water.")</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Why do we use water for 'drinking'?" (Expected answer: "Because it's essential for life.")</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "The Importance of Water".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short audio dialogue (or transcript read aloud by teacher) between two teenagers discussing their family's water conservation efforts and plans. The dialogue incorporates target vocabulary (e.g., <i>resource, tap, collect, waste, conserve, shortage</i>) and grammatical structures (first conditional, for + -ing, because). It is a short, conversational dialogue for an interview, casual register, focusing on practical actions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Interprets and produces oral texts related to daily life and personal experiences, using appropriate grammatical structures and vocabulary to express ideas about water conservation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students listen to the dialogue.
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> "What are the two friends talking about?" (Expected: saving water/water use).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False:</b> The teacher reads simple statements based on the dialogue. E.g., "One family collects rainwater." (T/F). "They waste a lot of water for cleaning." (T/F).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Match actions to reasons from the dialogue (e.g., "Turn off the tap" -> "save water because it's limited").</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">7-10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Speaking.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Students practice expressing future plans with the first conditional ("If we don't save water, we will face water shortages."), purpose of action ("We use water for drinking, cooking, and cleaning."), and cause-effect ("We save water because it is a limited resource.") using vocabulary like <i>potable, conserve, tap, collect, shortage</i>. Focus on stress patterns for compound words like 'rainwater'.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b>
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Sentence Completion (Controlled Practice):</b> Students work individually to complete sentences orally with the correct form of the verb or vocabulary word, then say them aloud. The teacher provides sentence starters. E.g., "If we (not waste) ______ water, we (have) ______ more." "We use water for (drink) ______, (cook) ______, and (clean) ______." "Water is an important (resource) ______ because ______." The teacher monitors for accurate use of the first conditional and 'for + -ing'.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Chain Drill (Semi-controlled):</b> Students sit in a circle. The first student starts a sentence with "If we..." related to water. The next student completes the sentence using the first conditional and then adds a "We use water for..." statement. The third student responds to the purpose or adds a "because" clause. E.g., S1: "If we collect rainwater..." S2: "...we will have water for cleaning. We use water for washing clothes." S3: "That's true because water is a limited resource." (Focus on interactive turn-taking and using target structures).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Discussion Prompts (Guided Interaction):</b> In pairs, students take turns asking and answering questions provided by the teacher, using the target language. Prompts: "What will happen if we waste a lot of potable water?" "What do you use water for at home, besides drinking?" "Why is it important to turn off the tap when brushing your teeth?" Partners provide peer feedback on clarity and use of key vocabulary and grammar.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "The Importance of Water".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task: "Our Water Conservation Project" Group Discussion.</b> Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each group discusses and plans a small "Water Conservation Project" for their home or school. They must:
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Discuss different ways they can save water daily.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Propose at least two specific actions or ideas for their project (e.g., "turn off the tap," "collect rainwater").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Explain why these actions are important, using "because" clauses (e.g., "We will collect rainwater because it is a free resource.").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Discuss what will happen if they implement these plans, using the first conditional (e.g., "If we turn off the tap, we will conserve a lot of water.").</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Finally, each group shares their "project" ideas with the class in a short, informal presentation (1-2 minutes per group), focusing on clear communication and collaborative interaction. The teacher encourages the use of target vocabulary (e.g., <i>conserve, collect, limit, potable, shortage, essential</i>) and structures.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The teacher uses an observation checklist during the group presentations. Students are assessed on their ability to interact in English, propose solutions to water conservation, and use the target language structures orally.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b>
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation (especially of multisyllabic words like 'important' and 'forecast').</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary related to water (e.g., <i>resource, tap, collect, conserve, shortage, potable</i>).</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate use of the first conditional ("If... will...") to discuss future plans.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate use of 'for + verb + -ing' to express purpose.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clear expression of arguments using 'because'.</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Collaboration and participation in group discussion.</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete a brief exit ticket with the following prompts:
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"One new idea I learned about saving water today is..."</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can now talk about saving water using (first conditional / 'because' / 'for -ing')." (Students choose one or more and complete the sentence).</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b>
                    <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of today's discussion about water was easy for me to talk about? What was difficult?"</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How can I practice speaking more about important topics like water conservation outside of class?"</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b> The group discussions allowed students to use the target language effectively in a collaborative setting. Most students were able to form simple arguments and express future plans. Some students still need more practice with the 'for + -ing' structure. I will prepare more sentence prompts for next time and provide a quick review of verb forms. I also need to ensure consistent monitoring of pronunciation, especially for longer words and stress patterns.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson's speaking activities and arguments will directly prepare students for the next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing Composition), where they will write short paragraphs detailing their water conservation projects and arguments for why clean water is important.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; vertical-align: top;">
                <p style="text-align: center;">3-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>